      "writtenOn": "2022-02-23T17:34:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "running through a possible scenario, where a Resource has one CB ref and one usage ref, and each is unref\u0027ed on separate threads. Assuming that a 0 CB ref count does *not* return the resource to the cache.\n\nIf we have two separate ref counts and the unref mutex:\n - the unref mutex is held over modification of the ref count and the call to notifyARefIsZero so the two unrefs will be processed in some exclusive ordering\n - if CB unref happens first, notifyARefIsZero\u0027s call to hasAnyRefs() returns true so no cleanup is performed; then usage ref happens and we attempt to return it to the cache; if that fails, we see there are no refs left (correctly) and delete it.\n - if the usage unref happens first, and the return to the cache is successful, we\u0027ve added it to the return queue. Importantly, nothing has changed on Resource to reflect that though, so when the CB unref is processed, it sees that hasAnyRefs() is false and actually deletes it. When the resource cache processes the return queue, we get into a bad state.\n \nIf we have a single atomic uint64_t and no mutex (see my other comments):\n - the initial ref value is 01 | 01\n - the usage unref does an atomic subtraction of 00 | 01 and the CB unref does an atomic subtraction of 01 | 00.\n - even with memory:acq_release, we should be safe to assume that the usage unref\u0027ing thread sees \u0027prior\u0027 \u003d\u003d 01|01 or 00|01. and the CB unref\u0027ing thread sees \u0027prior\u0027 \u003d\u003d 01|01 or 01|00.\n - it should not be the case that both threads see 01|01 as the prior value, though, so one thread will correctly detect that we no longer have any refs at all and could delete the object.\n - however, we do still have the issue where the usage unref\u0027ing thread returns the resource but the CB unref\u0027ing thread saw prior \u003d\u003d 01|00 and deletes the object entirely.\n \nIn either case, I think we need something marked on the Resource that says it\u0027s in the return queue. I wonder if this is just making the cache ref explicit instead of implicit? From the cache ref\u0027s POV, I think we\u0027d have that ref held whether or not it\u0027s in the return queue or the main arrays. We could have it as the lowest bit in the atomic ref counter and always just be 0 or 1, and usage refs shift by 1. Then full delete can safely happen on any thread when the new value is 0.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 34,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 34,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "4d9c90325ff7308599d262aca3d7a35020685228",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
