      "writtenOn": "2022-02-23T17:34:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Having a mutex and two atomics, and the mutex only being used on the unref (and not on the matching ref) feels brittle and/or overkill.\n\nI had the thought that maybe we can have a single atomic\u003cuint64_t\u003e that represents both types of refs. Encoding the ref types gets a little trickier, but I think the rest of the threading logic being simpler makes up for it.\n\nYou\u0027d have `std::atomic\u003cuint64_t\u003e fRefCnts;`\n\nAdding a usage ref would `fetch_add(+1, relaxed)` and a command buffer ref would `fetch_add(+1\u003c\u003c32, relaxed)`. Unreffing would subtract 1 or subtract 1\u003c\u003c32. hasAnyRefs() is trivially just checking if fRefCnts is 0, and hasUsageRef or hasCommandBufferRef uses fetch_and(upper/lower bit mask) to check each half of the 64 bit int. I don\u0027t think we\u0027d need a mutex at all then?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 173,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 177,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "4d9c90325ff7308599d262aca3d7a35020685228",
